[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Applying the node-type grid is a corpus migration, and three existing types have no ruling

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GRID-MIGRATION-UNCOSTED
TITLE: Applying the node-type grid is a corpus migration, and three existing types have no ruling
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID names six node types and the grammar declares five, and only
two names are common to both. Applying the grid is a CORPUS MIGRATION, not a
values.nix edit, and it has not been costed.

WHAT THE GRID DOES NOT SAY, which is a gap in that decision rather than a
subtlety of it. SLICE, INVARIANT and SPIKE appear nowhere in the grid, and the
decision never states their fate. Reading its own axes gives an answer for each,
but reading is not ruling:

  INVARIANT   normative and universal, so it IS a REQUIREMENT. The grid says a
              constraint is a requirement; it does not say the type collapses.
  SLICE       imperative. A unit of deliverable work makes no claim about the
              world, so it falls outside the grid the way WORK does.
  SPIKE       imperative likewise. A probe is work; what it produces is
              EVIDENCE. One type becomes two things at different times.

WHAT IT COSTS, measured against the corpus at 173 nodes on 2026-08-28. 117 are
MECHANISM, and the normative-or-descriptive test has to be applied to each ONE
AT A TIME -- it cannot be derived from any field, because the current grammar
never asked. At least one is already known to fail it: MECH-GRAMMAR-SGRA-NOT-
GENERATED is enforced by a check, which makes it normative, under a descriptive
prefix. Then 11 SLICE, 10 SPIKE and 4 INVARIANT by whatever the ruling above
turns out to be.

THE EXPENSIVE PART IS NOT THE TYPING. A node's UID carries its type prefix, so a
retype mints a new UID, and every relation naming the old one moves with it,
along with every PARENT_FP entry. The convention that a UID never changes -- the
thing that makes grooming safe today -- does not survive a retype, so the
migration needs a rule for identity across it before it needs a script.

GATED, not merely unscheduled. DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN decides whether execution
lives in beads, and if it does then SLICE and SPIKE may LEAVE the graph rather
than become WORK. Migrating before that is ratified risks doing it twice.

NOT A BLOCKER FOR THE HOOK LAYER. L2 is about the shape of a node -- its fields,
its relations, and the create, read, update and delete events over it -- and is
indifferent to which node types exist. The two can proceed in either order, and
hooks first costs nothing.

DO NOT HALF-APPLY IT. Adding the new types while existing nodes keep the old
ones leaves MECHANISM meaning two things at once, which is worse than either end
state and is not a smaller step.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The grid was accepted as a description of the target vocabulary and reads as though it were a grammar change. It is not. Recording the distance now keeps a later session from discovering mid-edit that a retype moves every citation, and keeps the three unaddressed types from being decided by whoever happens to touch them first.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Raised 2026-08-28, in the same session that wrote DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID, on the operator asking whether the rulings had been applied. They had not, and the question surfaced that the grid is silent on SLICE, INVARIANT and SPIKE. The grid's STATEMENT is not amended here: it describes the target correctly and its omission is about migration, which is this node's subject.
<<<
